ebruary 2006"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/159"} "Oval Lingotto, Torino"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/1122"} "37 from 12 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Two runs, total time determined placement."]] [:tr {:class "records", :style ""} [:th {:style "vertical-align: top;"} "Olympic Record "] [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/1108"} "34.42"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/99344"} "Casey FitzRandolph"] " " [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"] " / 11 February 2002 / " [:a {:href "/records/event_name/530"} "Details"]]] [:tr {:class "records", :style ""} [:th {:style "vertical-align: top;"} "Olympic Record  (2 races)"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/1108"} "69.23"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/99344"} "Casey FitzRandolph"] " " [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"] " / 11 – 12 February 2002 / " [:a {:href "/records/event_name/530/2%20races"} "Details"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Starter"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5003306"} "Luigi Casal"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Referee"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5004674"} "Tore Ramton"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Earlier in the Olympic season, the 500 m world record had been broken, for the first time in over 4 years. Japan’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/110051"} "Joji Kato"] " lowered the mark to 34.30, taking over the record from compatriot " [:a {:href "/athletes/97498"} "Hiroyasu Shimizu"] ". While also the 2005 World Champion, Kato had far from dominated the 2005/2006 season, with World Cup wins going to " [:a {:href "/athletes/99342"} "Jeremy Wotherspoon"] " (2003 and 2004 World Champion), " [:a {:href "/athletes/110052"} "Lee Gang-Seok"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/101371"} "Yu Fengtong"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/101364"} "Dmitry Dorofeyev"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/101360"} "Joey Cheek"] ". The latter two skaters had also surprisingly finished 1-2 at the most recent World Sprint Championships, winning one 500 m race each."] "\n\n" [:p {} "After the first run, the winner already seemed certain. American Joey Cheek was the only skater to beat 35 seconds (34.82), and led with a huge margin of 4 tenths over Dmitry Dorofeyev. The fight for bronze was close, with Lee (35.34), " [:a {:href "/athletes/110045"} "Yuya Oikawa"] " (35.35), Wotherspoon (35.37) and Yu (35.39) within 0.05 seconds of each other. World record holder Kato was back in 11th and out of medal contention."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The second run showed pretty much the same picture. Cheek was again the only one under 35 seconds (now with 34.94), and Dorofeyev consolidated his second place, despite being a bit slower than Lee. Cheek proved a popular Olympic champion, especially after he announced to give away his $25,000 award from the US Olympic Committee to charity (“Right to Play”, founded by " [:a {:href "/athletes/88548"} "Johann Olav Koss"] "). Medals won by Dorofeyev and and Lee were the first speed skating medals for their countries since 1994 and 1992, respectively. Dorofeyev’s 2006 successes were a remarkable departure from his earlier results, having only a 1999 World Cup victory as a notable prior achievement."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Co